{"file_path":"/home/john/mynotes/deterministic_equidistribution_theory/defect_cocycle_with_findings.tex","new_string":"Let $x_0$ have an infinite forward orbit, and suppose it violates the annealed law\npersistently: for some \\textbf{non-coboundary} $\\chi$ on some $\\Om_Q$, some tilt\n$s$, and some $\\delta>0$, $\\lvert W_n(x_0;\\chi,s)\\rvert\\ge\\delta$ along a\npositive-density set of $n$ (or, more weakly, along blocks of length\n$\\ell_j\\to\\infty$). Then there exist a finite pair $(Q,L)$ and a $\\chi$-sector\neigenstate of $\\Lcal_{s,Q,L,\\chi}$ with\n$\\lvert\\lambda\\rvert\\ge\\rho(\\Lcal_{s,Q,L,0})-o(1)$, exhibited by the orbit's own\nempirical measure. Conjoined with the gap\n$\\rho(\\Lcal_{s,Q,L,\\chi})\\le(1-\\kappa_Q)\\rho(\\Lcal_{s,Q,L,0})$ for non-coboundary\n$\\chi$, this is a contradiction. Hence every integer orbit equidistributes\nrelative to the annealed (valuation-pushforward) residue law.\n\\end{principlebox}\n\n\\begin{remark}[Coboundary refinement, forced by the computations]\nThe qualifier ``\\textbf{non-coboundary}'' is essential and is the one substantive\ncorrection the numerics forced. The faithful operator has exactly one structural\nzero-gap sector---the quadratic character $\\chi_2$---and it is \\emph{not} a defect\nbut an exact coboundary: $\\chi_2(x_i)=(-1)^{b_{i-1}}$ is valuation parity in\ndisguise (\\cref{app:quad}), so the residues are not uniform mod $3$ (they sit at\n$\\tfrac13:\\tfrac23$) and $\\chi_2$ belongs to the height/valuation \\emph{reference}.\nH23a is therefore not ``all nontrivial characters are gapped'' but\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\ \\text{all \\emph{non-coboundary} characters are gapped.}\\ }\n\\]\nAfter quotienting the (unique) coboundary, the gap is present, $Q$-uniform, and in\nfact constant (\\cref{app:quad}, \\cref{app:gap}). The positive-density hypothesis is\nalso likely too strong---obstructions may be sparse---so the principle is stated to\nadmit long blocks $\\ell_j\\to\\infty$ (the form used in Exp.~I).\n\\end{remark}","old_string":"Let $x_0$ have an infinite forward orbit, and suppose it violates the annealed law\npersistently: for some $\\chi\\ne1$ on some $\\Om_Q$, some tilt $s$, and some\n$\\delta>0$, $\\lvert W_n(x_0;\\chi,s)\\rvert\\ge\\delta$ along a positive-density set of\n$n$ (or, more weakly, along blocks of length $\\ell_j\\to\\infty$). Then there exist a\nfinite pair $(Q,L)$ and a $\\chi$-sector eigenstate of $\\Lcal_{s,Q,L,\\chi}$ with\n$\\lvert\\lambda\\rvert\\ge\\rho(\\Lcal_{s,Q,L,0})-o(1)$, exhibited by the orbit's own\nempirical measure. Conjoined with the gap\n$\\rho(\\Lcal_{s,Q,L,\\chi})\\le(1-\\kappa_Q)\\rho(\\Lcal_{s,Q,L,0})$, this is a\ncontradiction. Hence every integer orbit equidistributes.\n\\end{principlebox}\n\n\\begin{remark}\nThe positive-density hypothesis is likely too strong: Collatz-type obstructions\nmay be sparse, clustered, or survive only across scales. The principle should\nadmit long blocks $\\ell_j\\to\\infty$ rather than positive density---the form used\nin the numerical search (Part~III, Exp.~I).\n\\end{remark}","replace_all":false}